Net::LibSSH 0.003 (unreleased as of this ticket, repo at commit dfae6da) tightens three behaviours: (1) every Channel method except close() now croaks "...: channel is closed" instead of silently operating on self->channel==NULL after close(); (2) the session refcount chain now increments on the referent (SvRV) instead of the reference scalar, fixing a segfault when undef $ssh / $ssh = ... happened while a channel or sftp object was still alive; (3) disconnect() now invalidates every channel/sftp object opened on that session -- their methods croak "...: session was disconnected" (a distinct message from "channel is closed") instead of leaving dangling pointers into memory libssh already freed inside ssh_disconnect().

Checked this codebase (lib/Rex/Interface/Exec/LibSSH.pm, lib/Rex/Interface/Fs/LibSSH.pm, lib/Rex/Interface/File/LibSSH.pm, lib/Rex/Interface/Connection/LibSSH.pm) against all three:
  - every exit_status() call site reads it BEFORE close() (Exec/LibSSH.pm:90-91, Fs/LibSSH.pm:150-151 and 166-167) -- matches the supported order, so the new post-close croak does not fire on any current path.
  - disconnect() is called exactly once, at final teardown, in Connection/LibSSH.pm:98, and nothing touches a channel or sftp object afterward.

Conclusion: for the code as it stands today, all three changes are a pure improvement (loud croak instead of a silently wrong value or a segfault) with no call site that would newly break. No code change needed here beyond bumping the cpanfile pin (currently `requires "Net::LibSSH", "0.002";`) to 0.003 after it ships, so a fresh install picks up the fixes. Re-audit if new call sites are added that read exit_status() after close() or touch a channel/sftp object after disconnect() -- those would now croak instead of misbehaving quietly.

Filed by the net-libssh-release-checker pre-release audit of Net::LibSSH, checked against commit dfae6da (not yet released; karr #10 on that board is separately reworking disconnect()/connect() to make a spent session croak instead of timing out on reconnect -- worth re-checking this ticket once that lands too).



